From 0b153d655a7bd53318838cf1fd7ff1c68c78dc54 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: build <build@localhost>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 22:03:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Keep ties over page turns; tie into the right head of a
 chord; let a chord of another voice stand beside a tie

Ties came out lost or as slurs in three ways (piano-vocal scores, 9 songs):

1. Over a page turn. A tie (or slur) over a page turn is two halves: one at
   the end of the page's last system, one at the start of the next page's
   first system. Page.connectOrphanSlurs only connects halves between systems
   of one page, so these stayed with no extension, not ties (a tie needs both
   heads), hence abnormal, and LINKS step's checkAbnormals removed them all,
   before Voices.refineScore could connect them across pages. Now
   SlurInter.canStayAbnormal keeps such a page orphan (isPageOrphan: one head,
   beginning orphan of the page's first system or ending orphan of its last
   system); refineScore connects them as before (checkCrossTie: tie or slur)
   and then discards the page orphans that connected to nothing (start and end
   of the score, next to a missing sheet or part).

2. The wrong head at a curve end. Ties into the heads of a chord end between
   those heads, and two voices may share an abscissa; the head (or the chord)
   nearest the curve end is not always the tied one, so one curve of a tied
   chord got the lower head on one end and the upper head on the other, a
   slur. When the heads at the two ends are not tie-compatible, checkStaffTie
   (LINKS), and a second pass after checkCrossTie (Page.checkPageCrossTies,
   refineScore), look on each side for another head of the same staff right
   at the curve end (small abscissa gap, on the concave side within 1.25
   interline) that is tie-compatible with the far head and not already the
   end of another tie on that side; found, the curve is relinked to it and
   made a tie.

3. A chord of another voice struck with the tied head. isSpaceClear refused a
   tie when a chord intersected the tie's box, including a chord of another
   voice at the very abscissa of the left or right head. The final tie check
   in LINKS (checkStaffTie) now ignores chords struck along with either head;
   the early check at SLURS step (ClumpPruner) is unchanged: there, a tie into
   a chord from two chords makes ChordSplitter split that chord, whose rhythm
   then came out wrong.

Also, a cross-system connection is a tie only when no other head of the staff
lies between each head and its system edge (isEdgeClear; a head touching or
overlapping the tied head, e.g. a second, doesn't count): a phrase slur from a
note to a later note of the same pitch over the line break was read as a tie.

